after nearly two years of hardships and  sudden changes in the classroom ccsd  employees could get some extra money for  all their hard work a tentative  agreement was announced today to give  out retention bonuses for full-time  staff sasha loftus spoke with a local  educator tonight who hopes there's more  support on the way sasha joins us live  tonight with more  brian ccsd educators have been through a  lot over the past few years the teacher  i spoke with tonight said she's happy  with any extra compensation but she  believes more needs to be done  it's been a tough road for ccsd  employees since the start of the kovid  19 pandemic we have every hand on deck  that we can or we're  combining classes special education  teacher dolly rowan says with the latest  omicron search the hits just keep coming  we are trying our best but we cannot  keep those schools sanitized those  kiddos are not keeping their mask on  district leaders announced thursday that  a tentative agreement was reached to  provide retention bonuses to full-time  employees i'm always grateful for extra  income but it's a little too little too  late according to a memo sent to staff  anyone who was working full time as of  january 1st will receive 1 thousand  dollars those still on the books by may  25th are eligible for another thousand  rowan says it's a start but it doesn't  begin to cover out of pocket costs over  the last two years once i've already  endured so many costs during the  pandemic she says she and others hope to  see more support as those working both  full and part time do their best to keep  our valley kids safe  dr jarrah and district staff are set to  propose this at the next regular board  meeting which is next thursday january  13th now the board of trustees will have  to approve this in order for ccsd staff  to receive this money reporting live in  the newsroom tonight sasha loftis live  local  now
 

